.85, "cls": "instrument_malfunction", "label": "Telemetry or data-logging pipeline mirroring artifact", "rationale": "A correlation of exactly r = 1.000 across 112 paired observations between two physically distinct instruments (pendulum period in beats vs. resonator frequency in cycles/beat) at separate settlement sites is mathematically inconsistent with noisy physical processes. It strongly indicates a software, buffer, or telemetry pipeline defect directly copying or linearly transforming delayed pendulum_lab records into the resonator_obs datastream."}, {"run": "gemini/wd_exact-seed2004", "p": 0.8, "cls": "instrument_malfunction", "label": "Data pipeline or logging buffer artifact mirroring transformed readings with a 3-day delay", "rationale": "The reading-by-reading cross-correlation has now reached 1.000 across n=122 trials. A mechanical pendulum in the settlement lab and a resonator at the observatory operate on completely distinct physical principles; an exact deterministic linear mapping across separate modalities with zero dispersion or noise is essentially impossible in nature. This indicates a software, buffer, or telemetry artifact where resonator_obs is populated from a delayed transformation of pendulum_lab records."}, {"run": "haiku/wd_exact-seed2000", "p": 0.78, "cls": "instrument_malfunction", "label": "Data linkage artifact: pendulum_lab and resonator_obs share a common computational, logging, or database pathway; the r=1.000 correlation reflects duplicate data routing, field aliasing, time-shifted replication, or shared calibration pipeline rather than independent measurement.", "rationale": "Perfect correlation (r=1.000, n=102, \u00b10.287 chance) between nominally independent instruments in separate locations is physically implausible for true measurement streams. This singularity is unprecedented: all five other cross-instrument pairs show only noise-level or near-chance correlations (max observed r=0.268, min r=\u22120.264). The 3-day offset does not rescue this as a physical phenomenon; it implicates asynchronous batch processing or time-shifted data routing. Colocated resonator_obs shows only r=0.301 with pendulum_obs (1-day offset, n=120, chance \u00b10.265), and resonator_lab (identical instrument class to resonator_obs) shows only r=\u22120.241 with resonator_obs, ruling out environmental or procedural explanations for the pendulum_lab\u2013resonator_obs perfection. Today's fresh readings (6 pendulum_lab trials 305\u2013310, 6 resonator_obs trials 425\u2013436 in events 712586, 734548\u2013455395) remain too sparse to recompute the offset correlation meaningfully, but they do not yet break the pattern. This hypothesis is MANDATORY to resolve by data provenance audit: raw event logs for timestamp duplicates and source identity; database schema for field sharing or replication triggers; instrument control software for aliasing or shared calibration logic; explicit check whether resonator_obs readings were computed from pendulum_lab inputs or shared calibration data on days 0\u20133."}, {"run": "gemini/wd_exact-seed2001", "p": 0.75, "cls": "instrument_malfunction", "label": "Shared instrumentation pipeline, digitizer buffer replay, or software data stream artifact", "rationale": "An exact cross-correlation of r = 1.000 across 102 data points between two physically distinct instruments (a mechanical pendulum in the central lab and an optical/acoustic resonator at the observatory) separated by a 3-day lag is mathematically incompatible with physical propagation through a noisy medium. This demands an identical data stream, memory buffer replay, or shared deterministic synthesis routine in the acquisition pipeline."}]}